## formula
formula-mechanics-density = Density
formula-mechanics-density-variables-name = density
formula-mechanics-density-m-name = mass of the body
formula-mechanics-density-v-name = volume of the body
formula-mechanics-density-meaning-school = for an odd-shaped body — how much the water rose in the measuring cylinder
formula-mechanics-density-explanation-novice = Weigh the body and find how much room it takes. Divide one by the other — and you know what it may be made of.
formula-mechanics-density-explanation-school = The mass comes from the scales, the volume of an odd-shaped body from a measuring cylinder: the rise of the water is the volume of the body. ρ = m/V; a table of densities then tells the substance.
formula-mechanics-weight = Weight
formula-mechanics-weight-p-name = weight
formula-mechanics-weight-m-name = mass
formula-mechanics-weight-g-name = gravitational acceleration
formula-mechanics-weight-explanation-novice = The larger the mass, the harder the body pulls the dynamometer down.
formula-mechanics-weight-explanation-school = A 1 kg body at rest pulls on its hook with ≈ 9.8 N: P = m·g.
formula-mechanics-buoyant-force = Buoyant force
formula-mechanics-buoyant-force-f-a-name = buoyant force
formula-mechanics-buoyant-force-variables-name = density of the liquid
formula-mechanics-buoyant-force-v-name = volume of the submerged part
formula-mechanics-buoyant-force-explanation-novice = In water a body gets “lighter”: the water pushes it up. The denser the liquid, the stronger.
formula-mechanics-buoyant-force-explanation-school = In water a dynamometer reads less than in air: P′ = P − F_A. F_A equals the weight of the water in the volume of the submerged part.
formula-mechanics-submerged-share = Floating: the share of the body under the surface
formula-mechanics-submerged-share-variables-name = share under the surface
formula-mechanics-submerged-share-variables-name-2 = density of the body
formula-mechanics-submerged-share-explanation-novice = The lighter a body is compared with water, the more of it sticks out. Denser than water — it sinks.
formula-mechanics-submerged-share-explanation-school = A body floats when the buoyant force equals its weight: ρ_l·g·V_sub = ρ_b·g·V, hence V_sub/V = ρ_b/ρ_l. Ice (917 kg/m³) sits about 9/10 under water.
formula-mechanics-hooke = Elastic force (Hooke’s law)
formula-mechanics-hooke-f-name = elastic force
formula-mechanics-hooke-k-name = stiffness
formula-mechanics-hooke-x-name = extension
formula-mechanics-hooke-meaning-school = how far the body stretched: x = l − l₀
formula-mechanics-hooke-explanation-novice = The harder you pull a spring, the longer it gets — and it pulls back just as hard.
formula-mechanics-hooke-explanation-school = While the deformation is elastic, the extension is proportional to the force: twice the load, twice the extension. The ratio F/x is the stiffness k.
formula-mechanics-friction = Sliding friction
formula-mechanics-friction-f-name = sliding friction
formula-mechanics-friction-variables-name = coefficient of sliding friction
formula-mechanics-friction-n-name = normal force
formula-mechanics-friction-meaning-school = on a horizontal support it equals the weight of the body
formula-mechanics-friction-explanation-novice = The heavier the block, the harder it is to pull along the board.
formula-mechanics-friction-explanation-school = When the block is pulled at a steady speed, the dynamometer shows the friction. It is proportional to how hard the block presses on its support: F = μ·N. The coefficient μ depends on the materials and the finish of the surfaces.
formula-mechanics-pressure = Pressure
formula-mechanics-pressure-p-name = pressure
formula-mechanics-pressure-f-name = force
formula-mechanics-pressure-meaning-school = for a body on a horizontal support — its weight
formula-mechanics-pressure-s-name = area of support
formula-mechanics-pressure-explanation-novice = The same weight on a smaller area presses harder — that is why a sharp knife cuts and skis do not sink.
formula-mechanics-pressure-explanation-school = Pressure is the force on each square metre of support: p = F/S. A block stood on its smaller face presses harder, though its weight is the same.
formula-mechanics-hydrostatic-pressure = Hydrostatic pressure
formula-mechanics-hydrostatic-pressure-p-name = pressure of the liquid at the depth h
formula-mechanics-hydrostatic-pressure-h-name = depth
formula-mechanics-hydrostatic-pressure-meaning-school = from the surface of the liquid down to where the pressure is measured
formula-mechanics-hydrostatic-pressure-explanation-novice = The deeper, the more liquid above you — and the harder it presses.
formula-mechanics-hydrostatic-pressure-explanation-school = Each square metre at a depth h carries the weight of the column of liquid above it: p = ρ·g·h. The pressure does not depend on the vessel’s shape and is the same in all directions at the same depth (Pascal’s law).
formula-mechanics-bottom-force = The force of a liquid on the bottom
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-f-name = the force on the bottom
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-variables-name = the liquid’s density
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-g-name = the free-fall acceleration
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-h-name = the liquid’s height above the bottom
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-s-name = the bottom’s area
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-explanation-novice = However much water is in the vessel, it presses on the bottom as if only a straight column of water stood over the bottom.
formula-mechanics-bottom-force-explanation-school = The pressure at the bottom p = ρ·g·h, the force on the bottom F = p·S = ρ·g·h·S — the weight of the straight column over the bottom. It does not depend on the vessel’s shape.
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe = The atmospheric pressure from a syringe
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe-p-name = the atmospheric pressure
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe-f-name = the force pulling the shut syringe’s plunger
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe-l-name = the length of the syringe’s scale
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe-v-name = the volume that scale stands for
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe-explanation-novice = The plunger of a shut syringe is hard to pull: the air outside presses on it, and inside there is nothing to press back.
formula-mechanics-atmosphere-syringe-explanation-school = The air presses on the plunger with F = p·S. The scale gives its area: a cylinder of volume V and length l has a cross-section S = V / l. So p = F·l / V.
formula-mechanics-hydraulic = A hydraulic machine
formula-mechanics-hydraulic-f-name = force on the large piston
formula-mechanics-hydraulic-f-name-2 = force on the small piston
formula-mechanics-hydraulic-s-name = area of the small piston
formula-mechanics-hydraulic-s-name-2 = area of the large piston
formula-mechanics-hydraulic-explanation-novice = Push the small piston — the large one lifts a much heavier load.
formula-mechanics-hydraulic-explanation-school = The pressure under both pistons is the same (Pascal’s law): F₁/S₁ = F₂/S₂. The gain in force F₂/F₁ equals the ratio of the areas S₂/S₁ — the square of the ratio of the diameters. But the small piston travels as many times farther: there is no gain in work.
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels = Two liquids in communicating vessels
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels-variables-name = density of the second liquid
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels-variables-name-2 = density of the first liquid (water)
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels-h-name = height of the first liquid above the boundary
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels-h-name-2 = height of the second liquid above the boundary
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels-explanation-novice = A lighter liquid needs a taller column to balance a heavier one.
formula-mechanics-communicating-vessels-explanation-school = With one liquid the levels in communicating vessels are the same. Two liquids that do not mix stand so that their pressures are equal at the level of the boundary: ρ₁·g·h₁ = ρ₂·g·h₂ — the heights are inversely proportional to the densities.
formula-mechanics-speed = The speed of uniform motion
formula-mechanics-speed-v-name = speed
formula-mechanics-speed-l-name = path
formula-mechanics-speed-t-name = time
formula-mechanics-speed-explanation-novice = Uniform means the body covers the same path every second. Divide the path by the time — and you know how much.
formula-mechanics-speed-explanation-school = In uniform straight-line motion the path is proportional to the time: twice the time — twice the path, and their ratio v = l/t stays the same.
formula-mechanics-row-size = A small body’s size by the method of rows
formula-mechanics-row-size-d-name = the size of one body
formula-mechanics-row-size-l-name = the row’s length
formula-mechanics-row-size-n-name = the number of bodies in the row
formula-mechanics-row-size-explanation-novice = One grain is too small for a ruler. Lay many in a row, measure the whole row and divide by how many — you get the size of one.
formula-mechanics-row-size-explanation-school = The ruler’s error — half a division — is one for the whole row; divided by N, it is N times smaller for each body. The differences between the bodies average out the same way.
formula-mechanics-wound-length = A thread on a spool: length and turns
formula-mechanics-wound-length-l-name = the length of thread unwound (wound)
formula-mechanics-wound-length-d-name = the spool’s diameter
formula-mechanics-wound-length-n-name = the number of turns
formula-mechanics-wound-length-explanation-novice = In one turn of the spool the thread goes as far as the spool’s circumference. A thin spool has to turn more.
formula-mechanics-wound-length-explanation-school = The circumference C = π·d. In n turns the thread unwinds (winds) by n·π·d: that is how the load’s straight motion turns into the spool’s turning — and back.
formula-mechanics-kepler-third = Kepler’s third law
formula-mechanics-kepler-third-ta-name = the constant, the same for all the planets
formula-mechanics-kepler-third-t-name = the period (in the Earth’s years)
formula-mechanics-kepler-third-a-name = the distance from the Sun (in the Earth’s distances)
formula-mechanics-kepler-third-explanation-novice = A far planet not only has a longer orbit to go but also moves slower — that is why its year is so long.
formula-mechanics-kepler-third-explanation-school = If the distance from the Sun is a times larger, the planet’s year is √(a³) times longer: Jupiter is 5.2 times further from the Sun than the Earth — and goes round it in almost 12 years.
formula-mechanics-noon-latitude = The latitude from the noon Sun
formula-mechanics-noon-latitude-variables-name = the place’s latitude
formula-mechanics-noon-latitude-h-name = the Sun’s height above the horizon at noon
formula-mechanics-noon-latitude-variables-name-2 = the Sun’s declination (≈ 0° at an equinox)
formula-mechanics-noon-latitude-explanation-novice = The further north, the lower the noon Sun — and the longer the shadow.
formula-mechanics-noon-latitude-explanation-school = On an equinox the Sun stands over the equator: there at noon it is overhead (90°), and each degree of latitude north it stands a degree lower. On other days it is off the equator by its declination δ. The height comes from a stick’s shadow: h = arctan(H/L).
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift = How a calendar drifts from the Sun
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift-variables-name = how many days the equinox’s date moves (+ — later)
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift-n-name = how many years the calendar runs
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift-t-name = the calendar’s mean year, days
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift-t-name-2 = the year by the Sun — equinox to equinox: 365.24219 days
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift-explanation-novice = If the calendar year is shorter than the Sun’s, the dates run a little ahead of the seasons every year; if longer — behind.
formula-mechanics-calendar-drift-explanation-school = Every year the difference T☉ − T adds up: with 365 days — a quarter of a day a year, a day in four years; with the Julian (365.25) — a day in 128 years; with the Gregorian (365.2425) — a day in over 3000 years.
formula-mechanics-potential-energy = The potential energy of a raised body
formula-mechanics-potential-energy-e-name = potential energy
formula-mechanics-potential-energy-h-name = height
formula-mechanics-potential-energy-explanation-novice = The higher a body is raised and the heavier it is, the more work it can do as it falls.
formula-mechanics-potential-energy-explanation-school = Raising a body by h takes the work P·h = m·g·h; that much energy the body stores. Falling, it gives it back: potential energy turns into kinetic.
formula-mechanics-resultant = The resultant of two forces the same way
formula-mechanics-resultant-r-name = resultant
formula-mechanics-resultant-f-name = the first force
formula-mechanics-resultant-f-name-2 = the second force
formula-mechanics-resultant-explanation-novice = Two forces pull the same way — together they act like one force equal to their sum.
formula-mechanics-resultant-explanation-school = The resultant is the force that acts on a body as several forces do together. If two forces act along one line the same way, the resultant points the same way, and R = F₁ + F₂.
formula-mechanics-resultant-opposite = The resultant of two opposite forces
formula-mechanics-resultant-opposite-f-name = the larger force
formula-mechanics-resultant-opposite-f-name-2 = the smaller force
formula-mechanics-resultant-opposite-explanation-novice = Two forces pull opposite ways — the larger one “wins”, but only by the difference.
formula-mechanics-resultant-opposite-explanation-school = If two forces act along one line opposite ways, the resultant points the way of the larger, and R = F₁ − F₂. Equal opposite forces balance each other: R = 0.
formula-mechanics-average-speed = Average speed
formula-mechanics-average-speed-v-name = average speed
formula-mechanics-average-speed-t-name = time of motion
formula-mechanics-average-speed-explanation-novice = Divide the path by the time — and you know how many metres the body covered each second on average.
formula-mechanics-average-speed-explanation-school = A ball on a sloping groove speeds up, so its speed keeps changing. The average speed is the whole path over the whole time.
formula-mechanics-period = Period of turning (swinging)
formula-mechanics-period-t-name = period
formula-mechanics-period-n-name = number of turns (swings)
formula-mechanics-period-explanation-novice = One turn is too quick to catch with a stopwatch. So time ten turns and divide by ten.
formula-mechanics-period-explanation-school = The period is the time of one turn: T = t/N. The frequency is the other way round: n = N/t = 1/T.
formula-mechanics-pendulum = Period of a thread pendulum
formula-mechanics-pendulum-t-name = period
formula-mechanics-pendulum-l-name = length of the pendulum
formula-mechanics-pendulum-explanation-novice = A longer pendulum swings slower. A heavier ball or a wider swing does not change the rhythm.
formula-mechanics-pendulum-explanation-school = The period of small swings depends only on the length and g: a thread four times shorter — half the period.
formula-mechanics-moment = Moment of a force
formula-mechanics-moment-m-name = moment of a force
formula-mechanics-moment-f-name = force
formula-mechanics-moment-l-name = arm of the force
formula-mechanics-moment-meaning-school = the distance from the axis to the line of the force
formula-mechanics-moment-explanation-novice = The farther from the axis a load hangs, the harder it turns the lever.
formula-mechanics-moment-explanation-school = The moment M = F·l. A lever balances when the moments on both sides are equal: F₁·l₁ = F₂·l₂.
formula-mechanics-efficiency = Efficiency of a machine
formula-mechanics-efficiency-variables-name = efficiency
formula-mechanics-efficiency-a-name = useful work
formula-mechanics-efficiency-meaning-school = for a slope — lifting the load by h: P·h
formula-mechanics-efficiency-a-name-2 = work done
formula-mechanics-efficiency-meaning-school-2 = for a slope — pulling along it: F·l
formula-mechanics-efficiency-explanation-novice = Up a slope a load is easier to lift, but it has to be pulled farther — and part of the effort goes into friction.
formula-mechanics-efficiency-explanation-school = The efficiency of a slope η = P·h / (F·l): the useful work lifts the load by h, the work done pulls it along the slope of length l.
formula-mechanics-boyle = Boyle–Mariotte law
formula-mechanics-boyle-v-name = volume of the gas under the new pressure
formula-mechanics-boyle-v-name-2 = volume of the gas at first
formula-mechanics-boyle-p-name = pressure at first
formula-mechanics-boyle-p-name-2 = the new pressure
formula-mechanics-boyle-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = In grade 7 — qualitatively: a gas compresses easily, a liquid hardly at all; the law itself is beyond the grades 7–9 programme.
formula-mechanics-boyle-explanation-novice = Squeeze air — and it takes less room. Twice the pressure — half the volume.
formula-mechanics-boyle-explanation-school = If a gas is neither heated nor cooled (it is squeezed slowly), its pressure times its volume stays the same: p₁·V₁ = p₂·V₂. Liquids do not behave so: water under the same pressure compresses tens of thousands of times less.
formula-mechanics-torricelli = Outflow speed (Torricelli’s law)
formula-mechanics-torricelli-v-name = speed the liquid flows out at
formula-mechanics-torricelli-h-name = height of the liquid above the opening
formula-mechanics-torricelli-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = In grade 7 the fountain is built and explained qualitatively; the formula itself is beyond the grades 7–9 programme.
formula-mechanics-torricelli-explanation-novice = The higher the water stands above the opening, the faster it shoots out.
formula-mechanics-torricelli-explanation-school = A liquid flows out from under a height H as fast as a body would fall from the height H: the water’s potential energy above becomes kinetic at the opening.
formula-mechanics-rise-height = How high a body thrown up rises
formula-mechanics-rise-height-h-name = height risen
formula-mechanics-rise-height-v-name = upward speed at the start
formula-mechanics-rise-height-explanation-novice = The faster you throw it up, the higher it goes.
formula-mechanics-rise-height-explanation-school = At the top all the kinetic energy m·v²/2 has become potential m·g·h: m·v²/2 = m·g·h, so h = v²/(2g) — the mass cancels.
formula-mechanics-poiseuille = Head lost in a pipe (Hagen–Poiseuille)
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-h-f-name = height (head) lost
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-variables-name = viscosity of the liquid
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-l-name = length of the pipe
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-v-name = mean speed in the pipe
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-d-name = inner diameter of the pipe
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Viscous friction in pipes is beyond the school course; in grade 7 — qualitatively: part of the energy goes into friction.
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-explanation-novice = Water rubs on the pipe’s walls and on itself — and slows a little.
formula-mechanics-poiseuille-explanation-school = In a smooth (laminar) flow the head lost is proportional to the speed and the pipe’s length and inversely to the square of its diameter: a long narrow pipe eats a noticeable part of the height.
formula-mechanics-reynolds = The Reynolds number
formula-mechanics-reynolds-re-name = Reynolds number
formula-mechanics-reynolds-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Beyond the school course: it tells a smooth (laminar) flow from a churning (turbulent) one.
formula-mechanics-reynolds-explanation-novice = Slow water in a thin pipe flows smoothly, fast water in a wide one churns.
formula-mechanics-reynolds-explanation-school = Re compares a liquid’s inertia with its viscosity. In a round pipe the flow is laminar while Re is below ≈ 2300 and turbulent above ≈ 2900.
formula-mechanics-compressibility = Compressing a liquid
formula-mechanics-compressibility-v-name = volume of the liquid under pressure
formula-mechanics-compressibility-v-name-2 = volume of the liquid at first
formula-mechanics-compressibility-variables-name = compressibility of the liquid
formula-mechanics-compressibility-p-name = how much the pressure grew
formula-mechanics-compressibility-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = In grade 7 — qualitatively: liquids hardly compress, gases do easily; the compressibility as a number is beyond the programme.
formula-mechanics-compressibility-explanation-novice = Water can hardly be squeezed — which is why it passes pressure on so well.
formula-mechanics-compressibility-explanation-school = Under 100,000 Pa more (one more atmosphere) water shrinks by only ≈ 0.005 % — tens of thousands of times less than air. So a hydraulic machine moves its load at once, and a pneumatic one first squeezes its air.
formula-mechanics-work = Mechanical work
formula-mechanics-work-a-name = mechanical work
formula-mechanics-work-f-name = force acting along the motion
formula-mechanics-work-s-name = distance along the force
formula-mechanics-work-explanation-novice = Work is a force moving a body: the bigger the force and the farther it moved it, the more work.
formula-mechanics-work-explanation-school = If the force acts along the motion, the work A = F·s, in joules: 1 J is the work of 1 N over 1 m. Lifting a body we overcome its weight m·g over the height h: A = m·g·h, whatever the way up.
formula-mechanics-power = Power
formula-mechanics-power-n-name = power
formula-mechanics-power-a-name = work done
formula-mechanics-power-explanation-novice = Power is how fast work is done. Running up the same hill is more powerful than walking.
formula-mechanics-power-explanation-school = Power is work per unit of time: N = A/t, in watts: 1 W = 1 J/s. The same work in less time — more power.
formula-mechanics-kinetic-energy = Kinetic energy
formula-mechanics-kinetic-energy-e-name = kinetic energy
formula-mechanics-kinetic-energy-v-name = speed of the body
formula-mechanics-kinetic-energy-explanation-novice = The faster and heavier a body, the more energy of motion it has. Twice as fast — four times as much.
formula-mechanics-kinetic-energy-explanation-school = Kinetic energy is the energy of motion: Eₖ = m·v²/2. It grows with the square of the speed: twice as fast — 4 times the energy (which is why a car’s stopping distance grows so fast with speed).
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed = Speed of a ball that has rolled down
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed-v-name = speed at the bottom
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed-h-name = height rolled down
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed-k-name = share of the spin I/(m·r²)
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = The energy of rotation is beyond the grades 7–9 programme; in grade 7 — qualitatively: energy does not vanish, it is shared out.
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed-explanation-novice = A ball not only goes forward but also spins — and the spin takes energy too.
formula-mechanics-rolling-speed-explanation-school = Without slipping m·g·h = m·v²/2 · (1 + k): the spin takes the share k of the forward motion’s energy. For a solid ball k = 2/5, so only 5/7 ≈ 71 % of the energy goes forward.
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio = A gear pair
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio-n-name = turns of the driven gear
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio-n-name-2 = turns of the driving gear
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio-z-name = teeth of the driver
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio-z-name-2 = teeth of the driven
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio-explanation-novice = A small gear turns faster than a big one — as many times as it has fewer teeth.
formula-mechanics-gear-ratio-explanation-school = Meshing gears pass the same number of teeth by the meeting point: n₁·z₁ = n₂·z₂. So n₂ = n₁·z₁/z₂: from a big gear to a small one — faster, from a small to a big — slower. Each pair reverses the direction.
formula-mechanics-pulley = A pulley: the rope’s force and travel
formula-mechanics-pulley-f-name = force pulling the rope
formula-mechanics-pulley-p-name = weight of the load
formula-mechanics-pulley-n-name = rope sections holding the load
formula-mechanics-pulley-p-name-2 = weight of the movable pulley
formula-mechanics-pulley-explanation-novice = A fixed pulley only turns the force. A movable one halves the force, but the rope must be pulled twice as far.
formula-mechanics-pulley-explanation-school = The load is held by n sections of one rope, each with the same tension: F = P/n (with the movable pulley’s weight, (P + P_b)/n). But to lift the load by h, each section must shorten by h: s = n·h. There is no gain in work — the «golden rule» of mechanics.
formula-mechanics-lifting-force = The lifting force of a balloon
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-f-name = lifting force
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-variables-name = density of the air
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-variables-name-2 = density of the gas inside
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-v-name = volume of the balloon
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-m-name = mass of the shell
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-explanation-novice = The air pushes a balloon up, as water pushes a cork. If the gas inside with the shell is lighter than the same volume of air — the balloon flies.
formula-mechanics-lifting-force-explanation-school = The air’s buoyant force ρ_air·g·V acts on the balloon (Archimedes’ law). Less the weight of the gas ρ_gas·g·V and of the shell m_shell·g, that is the lifting force. It grows with the volume (the cube of the diameter), the shell’s weight only with its area — so big balloons lift more.
formula-mechanics-map-scale = A distance by the scale
formula-mechanics-map-scale-l-name = distance on the ground
formula-mechanics-map-scale-l-name-2 = distance on the map
formula-mechanics-map-scale-n-name = the scale’s denominator 1 : N
formula-mechanics-map-scale-explanation-novice = A scale of 1 : 1,000,000 means 1 cm on the map is 1,000,000 cm — 10 km on the ground.
formula-mechanics-map-scale-explanation-school = A map makes everything N times smaller, so the distance on the ground L = l·N. The smaller the scale (the bigger N), the rougher: a millimetre of the ruler is N millimetres of ground.
formula-mechanics-great-circle = The distance between towns over the Earth
formula-mechanics-great-circle-l-name = shortest distance over the surface
formula-mechanics-great-circle-variables-name = latitude of the first place
formula-mechanics-great-circle-variables-name-2 = longitude of the first place
formula-mechanics-great-circle-variables-name-3 = latitude of the second place
formula-mechanics-great-circle-variables-name-4 = longitude of the second place
formula-mechanics-great-circle-r-name = radius of the Earth
formula-mechanics-great-circle-mechanics-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Beyond the school course: this is how navigators and airlines reckon the distance between towns.
formula-mechanics-great-circle-explanation-novice = The Earth is round, so the shortest way between towns is an arc on the ball.
formula-mechanics-great-circle-explanation-school = From two places’ latitudes and longitudes one finds the angle between them at the Earth’s centre; the arc is that angle times the radius.
